<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chełmno law</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1233</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Kulm law, Culm law, Kulmer Handfeste</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Chełmno</a> law, German Kulm law, was the town law granted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> in its charter of 1233 for the towns of Chełmno and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a>. Based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> law, it gave towns self-government and settlers secure property on favourable terms. It became the model for the founding charters of most towns and many villages in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and was later used widely in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chelmno-law.jpg" alt="Chełmno law" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sławomir Milejski · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SM_Che%C5%82mno_Ratusz_(4)_ID_601491.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The law was issued on 28 December 1233 in the Monastic State of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> by Grand Master Hermann von Salza and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-balk">Hermann Balk</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a> (Thorn) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Chełmno</a> (Kulm) received German town law as a modification of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> rights. The charter, the Kulmer Handfeste, was named after the town where it was signed. The original was lost in 1244, when the town hall of Chełmno burned during an attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swietopelk-ii-duke-of-pomerania">Świętopełk II, Duke of Pomerania</a>. A renewed charter of 1 October 1251 was based on a copy kept in Toruń, but with reduced rights.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Compared with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> rights the law gave residents several advantages, among them the right to elect the local judge and inheritance by descendants of both sexes. It was granted mostly by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> to towns of its state and by the neighbouring Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>, but also elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and from the 13th to the 18th century at least 224 towns received it. The town hall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Chełmno</a> was the seat of the higher court of the law until 1458. Independently of the Knights, the law was also expanded into a larger collection known as the Alter Kulm.</p><p>The towns founded under the law now lie mostly in northern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobrzyn-land">Dobrzyń Land</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masuria">Masuria</a>, Mazovia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> and Warmia among other regions, with some in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s Kaliningrad Oblast. The largest are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> cities of Płock, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a> and Grudziądz, the ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Kaliningrad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Klaipėda</a>, and Olsztyn.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the German occupiers stole the charter of 1251 from the Old Town City Hall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a>, but it was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> after the war and has been held in the State Archive in Toruń since 1959. It is on the Polish National List of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/memory-of-the-world-programme">Memory of the World Register</a>. The historic centres of Toruń and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>, and those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Chełmno</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a> and Tykocin are among the Historic Monuments of Poland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kulm law” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prussia's free peasants, the Kölmer, held free estates under <b>Culm law</b> with about a sixth of the arable land, much more than in other lands of the feudal era.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kulm law</b> guaranteed inheritance also in the female line, which led to the fragmentation of estates; in 1522 the nobility demanded the Polish model of inheritance and land ownership.</blockquote><a href="/empire/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Kulm charter</b> of 1233, towns received self-government and settlers held land on favourable terms, and from the late thirteenth century thousands of peasants from Silesia, Saxony and other German lands founded villages laid out by contractors known as locators.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Chełmno law</b>, granted in the Culm charter of 1233, became the model for town and land rights across Prussia.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-culm">Bishopric of Culm</a></li></ul></section>
